WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.295
Lesson 5.2.

00:00:02.295 --> 00:00:05.490
Layer 2s, sequencers & withdrawal paths.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:07.685
By the end of this lesson, you'll be

00:00:07.685 --> 00:00:10.295
able to understand how your L2 settles, who

00:00:10.295 --> 00:00:12.965
orders your transactions, and how you'd exit.

00:00:14.265 --> 00:00:15.555
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.555 --> 00:00:17.240
First, the key ideas.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.955
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:19.955 --> 00:00:21.480
Then, your checklist.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:23.642
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:24.942 --> 00:00:28.434
First, Rollups execute transactions off Ethereum and

00:00:28.434 --> 00:00:30.777
post data or proofs back to it.

00:00:30.777 --> 00:00:35.972
Next, Optimistic rollups assume transactions are valid unless challenged.

00:00:35.972 --> 00:00:39.564
Native withdrawals to Ethereum wait out a challenge

00:00:39.564 --> 00:00:43.317
window (about 7 days on major optimistic rollups).

00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:45.842
Then, ZK rollups post validity proofs,

00:00:45.842 --> 00:00:48.472
so native withdrawals can be faster.

00:00:48.472 --> 00:00:52.492
Also, Sequencer: the operator that orders transactions.

00:00:52.492 --> 00:00:54.783
Often a single operator today; if it

00:00:54.783 --> 00:00:57.002
goes down, the chain may pause.

00:00:57.002 --> 00:01:00.680
And finally, Escape hatches: most rollups let you force

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:04.592
a withdrawal via Ethereum if the sequencer censors you.

00:01:04.592 --> 00:01:05.822
Know if yours does.

00:01:07.123 --> 00:01:09.638
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:09.638 --> 00:01:12.151
You hold $20,000 on an optimistic

00:01:12.151 --> 00:01:14.743
rollup and need it on Ethereum.

00:01:14.743 --> 00:01:17.828
Native bridge: safest, ~7 days.

00:01:17.828 --> 00:01:19.525
Fast bridge: minutes, for a

00:01:19.525 --> 00:01:21.643
fee, relying on its liquidity.

00:01:21.643 --> 00:01:24.087
Plan your liquidity ladder (Module

00:01:24.087 --> 00:01:25.973
12.4) around the slow path.

00:01:27.273 --> 00:01:28.733
Here's your checklist.

00:01:28.733 --> 00:01:30.268
Do it now, for real.

00:01:30.268 --> 00:01:32.874
First, Rollup type and withdrawal time

00:01:32.874 --> 00:01:34.778
known for each L2 I use.

00:01:34.778 --> 00:01:37.813
Next, Sequencer setup and escape hatch known.

00:01:37.813 --> 00:01:40.713
And finally, Liquidity plan assumes the slow path.

00:01:42.013 --> 00:01:43.223
Question 1.

00:01:43.223 --> 00:01:46.866
Why do optimistic rollup withdrawals take ~7 days?

00:01:51.286 --> 00:01:54.106
The answer: The challenge window lets anyone

00:01:54.106 --> 00:01:56.801
dispute an invalid state before it's final.

00:01:58.101 --> 00:01:59.346
Question 2.

00:01:59.346 --> 00:02:01.951
What happens if a single sequencer goes down?

00:02:06.371 --> 00:02:07.917
The answer: The L2 may

00:02:07.917 --> 00:02:10.403
pause transactions until it recovers.

00:02:11.703 --> 00:02:12.973
Question 3.

00:02:12.973 --> 00:02:14.308
What's an escape hatch?

00:02:18.728 --> 00:02:20.499
The answer: A way to force a

00:02:20.499 --> 00:02:23.247
transaction or withdrawal via the base chain

00:02:23.247 --> 00:02:25.018
if the sequencer censors you.

00:02:26.318 --> 00:02:27.563
Let's recap.

00:02:27.563 --> 00:02:30.928
Rollups execute transactions off Ethereum and post

00:02:30.928 --> 00:02:32.943
data or proofs back to it.

00:02:32.943 --> 00:02:37.595
Optimistic rollups assume transactions are valid unless challenged.

00:02:37.595 --> 00:02:39.912
ZK rollups post validity proofs, so

00:02:39.912 --> 00:02:42.335
native withdrawals can be faster.

00:02:42.335 --> 00:02:45.425
Sequencer: the operator that orders transactions.

00:02:46.725 --> 00:02:49.240
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:53.565
Next up, Lesson 5.3: Oracles, TWAPs & manipulation.
